---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { CONTACT_URL, MINIMUM_AGE, TERMS_EFFECTIVE } from '../lib/legal';
---

<Base title="Privacy">
  <div class="prose-page">
    <header class="hero">
      <h1 class="page-title">Privacy notice</h1>
      <p class="micro-label">Effective {TERMS_EFFECTIVE}</p>
      <p class="lede">
        What this site stores about you, why, who else can see it, and how to get rid of it.
        Written from the code rather than from a template, and not legal advice. If you run
        ResearchScout yourself, everything below stays on the machine you run it on.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="block">
      <h2>Who is responsible</h2>
      <p>
        The site is operated by one person as a personal, non-commercial project. There is no
        company behind it. Reach the operator through{' '}
        <a href={CONTACT_URL} rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">the project's issue
        tracker</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="block">
      <h2>What is collected</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Your account.</strong> An identifier from the sign-in provider, and a display
          name if you set one. Not your email address: the provider holds that, and the token
          this site receives does not carry it. Passwords are handled entirely by the provider
          and never reach this site.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>What you keep.</strong> Papers you save and the research interests you type
          into your profile.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>What you read here.</strong> Which papers were shown to you, which you opened,
          how long you stayed on one, which you dismissed, and when. This is what makes the
          personalized feed work.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Questions you ask Scout.</strong> Fast answers are assembled from the stored
          papers and involve no model provider. AI answers send your question, with short
          excerpts of the matched papers, to a hosted language model to write the reply. A
          truncated copy of the question is stored with timing information to diagnose slow
          answers.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Ordinary server records.</strong> Requests reach the site through hosting and
          network providers that log IP addresses and request details for security and abuse
          prevention, as any web service does.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        There are no analytics, no advertising, no tracking pixels, no third-party fonts or
        scripts, and nothing is sold or shared for marketing. The only cookie is the one that
        keeps you signed in, which is why the site does not ask for cookie consent.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="block">
      <h2>Why it is collected</h2>
      <p>
        The account exists to give you a reading list, interests and settings, which is the
        service you asked for when you registered. Reading signals exist to rank the feed for
        you, which is the legitimate interest of making a research radar useful; you can delete
        them by deleting your account. Server records exist to keep the site up and to stop
        abuse.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="block">
      <h2>Who else sees it</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Auth0</strong> handles registration, sign-in and password resets.</li>
        <li><strong>Vercel</strong> serves the web pages.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>Railway</strong> hosts the backend and the database, in the United States.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A language model provider</strong> receives the questions you ask Scout in AI
          mode, together with short excerpts of the matched papers, to generate the answer.
          When the service runs on a provider's free model tier, that provider may log those
          prompts under its own terms - ask nothing in AI mode you would not put in a search
          box.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Sentry</strong> receives error reports - the technical details of a page or
          request that broke, such as the failing URL and a stack trace - so faults get seen
          and fixed. Error reports are not profiling and carry no reading history.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Each of those sees what it needs to do its job, under its own terms. Nobody else does.
        Your saved papers, interests and reading history live in the service's database and its
        backups, and are not shared with anyone.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="block">
      <h2>How long it is kept</h2>
      <p>
        Account data and reading history are kept while the account exists. Deleting your
        account removes the login at the sign-in provider and every row tied to you - saved
        papers, interests and reading events - immediately, in one step. Backups roll over
        within a week. Questions stored for timing are truncated and carry no account
        identifier.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="block">
      <h2>Your rights</h2>
      <p>
        From your <a href="/profile">profile</a> you can see what is stored, correct your
        display name, download everything as JSON, and delete your account and its data. If you
        are in the UK or the EU you also have the right to object to processing, to ask for a
        copy in a portable form (the export does this), and to complain to your data protection
        authority. Ask through the{' '}
        <a href={CONTACT_URL} rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">issue tracker</a> and it
        will be handled.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="block">
      <h2>Children</h2>
      <p>
        The site is not for anyone under {MINIMUM_AGE}. No account should be created for a
        child, and any that is found will be deleted.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="block">
      <h2>Where papers come from</h2>
      <p>
        The research data on the site is not personal data: it comes from arXiv, Semantic
        Scholar and the other sources listed on the <a href="/about">about page</a>, which
        explains what is taken from each and under what terms. Opening the reader makes your
        browser fetch the PDF from arXiv directly, so arXiv sees that request as it would any
        visit.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="block">
      <h2>Changes</h2>
      <p>
        If this notice changes in a way that matters, the terms version is bumped and the site
        asks you to read and accept it again on your next visit.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>

